abstract: >-
  A browser-native training runtime that steps an assembly procedure one
  operation at a time beside a live exploded view of the component each step
  describes, with knowledge checks posed against that same geometry. Every
  part, with its bores, lead-in chamfers, relief groove and knurled cap, is
  generated as a turned profile and revolved procedurally rather than
  modelled, so a feature the training text depends upon, such as a seal ring's single
  chamfered face, exists as geometry that catches a real highlight. Rendering
  uses WebGPU with TSL node materials and falls back to WebGL2 through the
  same node graph. The application makes no external network requests at
  runtime; fonts and the three.js build are served from the repository and a
  headless check fails the build on any off-origin request.

  Scope, stated as part of the record: the module content is a FIXTURE. There
  is no customer, no production line and no measured outcome anywhere in this
  work. The readiness figures, operator roster and module library are
  invented, and where a delta is shown it compares two points within that
  invented series rather than reporting an effect. The authoring pipeline
  presented in the interface is a storyboard on a timer: no document, image or
  CAD body is parsed, matched or tessellated. No claim is made or implied
  about training effectiveness, defect reduction or time to competence.
